Author: Oliver Kurz <okurz@suse.de>
Date:   Tue Oct 5 12:01:14 2021 +0200

    Use consistent "Copyright" header without problematic characters
    
    Same as done for os-autoinst in
    https://github.com/os-autoinst/os-autoinst/pull/1799 and openQA in
    https://github.com/os-autoinst/openQA/pull/4265
    
    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copyright_symbol
    explains that (c) can be used as a commonly accepted alternative to the
    non-ASCII character previously used which can sometimes (still) cause
    problems which we can avoid.
    
    According to https://stackoverflow.com/a/221380 even the character
    itself is seen as redundant as a repetition of the word "Copyright".
    Also in the United States, the Berne Convention Implementation Act of
    1988, effective March 1, 1989, removed the requirement for the copyright
    symbol from U.S. copyright law so it is not necessary after all.
    
    So we could have selected "(c)" or treat all files as unicode and
    convert any ISO8859-1 copyright characters to the according unicode
    character symbol or just get rid of the character at all.
    
    This commit removes all variants of copyright characters in our source
    files excluding binary files, full-text license files or external
    references, i.e. os-autoinst-common.
    
    I used the command
    
    ```
    git grep -l 'Copyright' | xargs sed -i 's/Copyright \((C)\|(c)\|©\)/Copyright/g'
    ```
    
    and manually reverted the changes in places where the change was
    unintended as stated above.
    
    This helps to not upset TidyAll and also seems to be a more consistent
    standard among source files in multiple projects.
    
    Related progress issue: https://progress.opensuse.org/issues/98616

